  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/medicine_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B005",
        "laureates": [
          "Kazutoshi Mori",
          "Peter Walter"
        ],
        "citation": "for discoveries of the unfolded protein response and signaling from the endoplasmic reticulum"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B007",
    "B013"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I have changed my preferred configuration to B005. The unfolded protein response is a fundamental, conserved, and mechanistically mature biological system with wide physiological and medical relevance. Mori and Walter provide a coherent two-person attribution for complementary foundational advances in ER-stress signaling. On reassessment, this defined mechanism has a more defensible discovery boundary than my prior preferred B007 configuration.",
    "case_against": "UPR research encompasses a broad field, and the historical boundary must remain restricted to the foundational signaling discoveries rather than later proteostasis work. Its clinical consequences are less direct than those of TNF blockade or GLP-1 therapies, which remain important counterweights in comparing merit.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "sandberg-rickard",
        "point": "Sandberg argued that B005 combines a mature, conserved mechanism with a compact attribution, while recognizing that later UPR work involved many contributors.",
        "response": "I find this delimitation persuasive. The appropriate citation is narrowly framed around discovery of the UPR and ER signaling, not the whole proteostasis field. That limitation makes the Mori-Walter configuration stronger than an award framed as recognition of all subsequent UPR research."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "linnarsson-sten",
        "point": "Linnarsson favored B005 because its mechanistic boundary and two-person attribution are clearer than those of candidates built on extended translational pathways.",
        "response": "I agree after reconsideration. B007 remains an exceptionally mature foundational alternative, but its proposed attribution must be separated from a wider historical sequence in immunology. B005 presently offers the firmer combination of conceptual importance, maturity, and creditable scope."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "wahren-herlenius-marie",
        "point": "Wahren-Herlenius emphasized the durable therapeutic transformation of TNF blockade and the focused Feldmann-Maini attribution.",
        "response": "B013 remains a compelling alternative because its medical benefit is unusually clear and longstanding. I give B005 a narrow edge because the proposed citation identifies an integrated foundational cellular mechanism, while TNF biology and biologic-treatment development still require careful separation from the defined clinical achievement."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether a historical review would confirm that Mori and Walter alone sufficiently delimit the foundational UPR signaling discovery.",
      "Whether the B- and T-lymphocyte lineage discovery can be credited with comparable precision to Miller and Cooper despite its broader developmental history.",
      "How much direct therapeutic transformation should outweigh a more sharply delimited fundamental mechanism."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
